// The proof of `number_does_pre_and_post_is_constant` — the contract claim:
// either the precondition fails, or the answer satisfies the postcondition.
//
// This used to be the corpus's hardest proof: a page of hand-derived cuts,
// because its then arm is reached only when `is_tag` held — a fact that is
// a *disjunction* (`v = t1` or `v = t2`) no rule window could spend, so the
// case analysis had to be written out waypoint by waypoint. The diagram
// engine builds that case analysis itself: branches become an ordered case
// tree, the arm that tested equal to a literal holds the literal, and each
// leaf folds on the machine. What the proof has left to say is the one
// thing the engine never does on its own — spend the definitions — and
// which wire each split is about.
//
// It says that by address, the way every other step of this language says
// where: `#zl` is the test `v = t1`, and `#y`, inside the case where that
// one failed, is `v = t2`. Both are read off the listing the goal prints
// when the step in front of the split is `exact`. The goal holds four
// `equal`s, and which of them a split means is not something the shape of
// the step could decide — the outermost is the postcondition's own tuple
// guard, and splitting there decides nothing.
//
// The tree is the split's own: a `cases` is η on its wire and then the
// branch that makes spent by `select-same`, so each case is an
// independent goal written inside it. Both leaves of `#zl`'s true case
// fold on the machine; the false case is where the second tag is still
// open, so the second split lives there.
proof types_test::number_does_pre_and_post_is_constant =
    inline both(decide)
    cases(#zl) (
        true: diagram,
        false: both(decide) cases(#y) (true: diagram, false: diagram));
